{
  "abstract": "Finite quadratic jet determinants lead to a universal primitive lattice\nwhose volume depends on compatibility between residue disks. We determine\nits leading quadratic coefficient. For $q=p^2$, $c=(p-1)/p$, and\n$J_p=\\int_0^1 x^{c-1}/(q-x)\\,dx$, the signed index satisfies\n$\\beta_N/N^2\\to 1/[2(q-1)J_p]-c/2$. In particular the dyadic constant is\n$1/(3\\log3)-1/4=0.05341307554227913\\ldots$. The upper bound retains\nthe complete image of integral polynomials under hierarchical Hasse\nderivative evaluations. A weighted-image argument and Hermite interpolation\npreserve that image through arbitrary derivative gaps and residue\nclustering, with an explicit logarithmic degree buffer and\n$O_d(N\\log N)$ determinant error. Common-primitive elimination transfers\nthe resulting minors to the actual restricted Newton source. For the\nmatching lower bound, repeated Taylor expansion bounds every weighted\nminor by a flow on the residue tree. Explicit vertex potentials retain\nthe origin prefix, derivative prices, and global degree constraint. They\ngive a finite rational certificate with error at most\n$3N\\lfloor\\log_p(5N+6)\\rfloor$. Both bounds have quadratic energies\nevaluated by the same moment profile. The previously established\nall-degree convergence theorem converts the matching liminf bounds into\nthe displayed limit. Explicit parameter rounding and growing-depth error\nestimates also give an absolute normalized error at most\n$10\\lfloor\\log_p(8N)\\rfloor/\\sqrt N$ for\n$N\\ge\\max(65536,p^4)$. Ten exact control suites check interpolation images,\nnormalized minors, Taylor factors, potential inequalities, lattice\nnormalizations, and rational enclosures. At depth four and degree $10^6$,\nthe lower certificate gives $\\beta_N/N^2>0.0533383214072$. The result\nsupplies finite lower universal-index credit and closes the preceding\nasymptotic gap. No new discriminant endpoint, census, or classification\nis claimed.",
